  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Arrest in execution of a civil obligation</h3>
    <p>Triggered by default on an adjudicated liability — unpaid maintenance under Section 144(3) BNSS, a money decree under Order XXI CPC, or civil contempt. The person is a defaulter, not an accused. The remedy is payment, security, instalments, or a challenge to the underlying order — not bail.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>How Section 144(3) BNSS operates</h2>
<p>Once a maintenance order exists and instalments go unpaid, the claimant applies for execution. The provision then works in a sequence courts apply strictly. First, the application must be made within one year of the amount falling due — the familiar one-year rule for arrears carried over from Section 125(3) CrPC. Second, the Magistrate issues a warrant for levying the amount due, in the manner of levying fines — which reaches the defaulter's movable property. Third, if the amount or part remains unrecovered, the Magistrate may sentence the defaulter to imprisonment up to one month for each month's allowance remaining unpaid, or until payment is sooner made. Throughout, the defaulter is entitled to show "sufficient cause" for the failure — loss of employment, illness, or payments actually made — and to be heard before coercive orders issue.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>The key conceptual point:</strong> the detention is a lever, not a punishment. That is precisely why the anticipatory bail jurisdiction — designed to protect the presumption of innocence of a person facing a criminal accusation — has no purchase here. There is no innocence to presume; the liability has been adjudicated, and the only questions are payment and sufficient cause.</p>
</div>

<h2>What the defaulting respondent should do instead</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appear, do not abscond.</strong> Evasion converts a negotiable arrears problem into non-bailable warrants and, eventually, proclamation. Appearance with a payment proposal changes the court's disposition immediately.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reconstruct the account.</strong> File a tabulated statement of every payment made with bank proof. Execution applications often claim gross arrears without crediting part-payments.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Seek instalments.</strong> Magistrates routinely spread genuine arrears over realistic instalments, keeping the warrant in abeyance subject to compliance.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Challenge where challenge lies.</strong> If the order itself is vulnerable — passed ex parte without service, or fixed on an income the respondent never had — pursue recall or revision and seek interim protection there, usually on condition of deposit. Changed circumstances go to an application under Section 146 BNSS, not to defiance of the existing order.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the claimant should know</h2>
<p>For the wife or parent holding the order, Section 144(3) is only one tool. Arrears can also be pursued against salary through the employer, and a respondent who hides income can be confronted with the asset-and-income affidavit regime the courts now enforce in maintenance litigation. The one-year rule rewards promptness: execution should be filed as arrears accrue, not saved up for years. And where the respondent offers instalments, a realistic schedule backed by the threat of a live warrant usually recovers more, faster, than insistence on immediate detention.</p>

<h2>The adjacent cases</h2>
<p>The same boundary disposes of two neighbouring confusions. Detention of a judgment-debtor in execution of a money decree under the CPC is civil process, governed by the CPC's own safeguards about means and conduct — anticipatory bail has no role. And a person facing possible detention for civil contempt answers to the contempt jurisdiction itself, including by purging the contempt, not to the bail provisions of the BNSS. In all three situations the instinct to file for anticipatory bail is understandable and wrong; the money, and the court holding the order, are where the solution lies.</p>

